The loader accepted the stricter manifest/evidence invariants; the authenticated readiness accepted the stricter closed contract. A before/after byte hash assertion proved the existing reference remained unchanged. `git merge-base --is-ancestor` also confirmed that its source HEAD is an ancestor of this fix commit.

### Prohibited Paths and Cleanup

- No hidden prices, returns, metrics, outcomes, or objectives were evaluated or inspected.
- No provider/OpenRouter, network, Docker, replay, capture, upload, push, merge, cloud, or original-checkout write occurred.
- Both supplied external inputs remained read-only; the 68 MB bundle was not opened or copied during this round.
- Every test/validation command ran singly in the foreground. No background Python process was started or left by this round.
- The tracked worktree was clean after commit; ignored artifact/report files remain outside Git.

### Self-Review and Concerns

- The ancestry gate precedes bundle authentication/evaluation; both low-level equality protection and repository ancestry protection fail before persistence.
- Re-authentication uses the same clean-source routine as the initial identity, so a non-ignored untracked file, staged/unstaged change, HEAD move, or tree-fingerprint change fails closed.
- The unavoidable filesystem interval between the final identity check and create-only open is kept to the immediately adjacent call; no evaluation or other application work occurs between them.
- Readiness validation delegates producer rules to `_readiness_identity` and supplements it only with closed outer fields and externally fixed/cross-linked authorities, minimizing duplicated policy logic.
- The only concern is the unrelated broader canary mutation test fixture/regex incompatibility documented above. The required focused suites and the narrow readiness validator tests relied upon are green.
